# Clarify the lifetime of a held live genesis

Ruling 70186a25 item 6 says a live scope reads sender genesis once and holds
it. Does this permit the following bounded Scope-instance interpretation?

The receiver reuses an already checked source genesis at position 0, or
makes one existing bounded exact-proof read on a cold miss. It holds the
immutable checked bytes in a cache keyed by full SequenceRef, bounded by
32 frames and 8 MiB at current entry bounds. Completed frames can leave
by bounded eviction; restart loses the instance cache. A later use then
makes a fresh exact read and verifies the same genesis again. Concurrent
cold inputs may duplicate a bounded read; no pending map or queue exists.
No cache hit supplies authority, Native birth proof or from.pin evidence.

This means once while held, not one remote read for the room's permanent
lifetime. It adds no durable cache payload, SQL row or archive export and
keeps the existing Native retained-byte/reservation ledger unchanged.

If item 6 instead requires permanent durable retention and one read across
restarts, that is a different construction. Ordinary retained birth uses
need explicit byte reservations and archive/privacy treatment; an unnamed
private retained blob cannot be added without breaking current Replay byte
accounting. The builder will not choose that larger construction silently.

This question concerns live cache lifetime only. The caller-only offline
record in 70186a25 and the after-checker-answer condition of 14493009 stay
unchanged. No code or validation ran.
